# THE KILL SWITCH
#
# Replace the word above with `disabled` (or delete this file) and every agent-authored
# change is blocked at CI. One edit, one commit, no configuration, no coordination.
#
# The first line is the only line that is read. Everything below is commentary.
#
# WHY A FILE AND NOT A SETTING
#
# It is in the repository, so it is versioned, reviewable, and revertible. It works
# from a phone over the GitHub web editor. It needs no credentials, no dashboard, and
# no working local environment — which matters because the moment you need it is
# probably a moment when something else is already broken.
#
# WHAT IT DOES AND DOES NOT DO
#
# Blocks: any commit marked agent-authored (branch `agent/*` or an `Agent-Implemented:`
# trailer) from passing CI. Since agent work reaches main only via a PR a human merges,
# and CI gates that merge, this stops agent changes landing.
#
# Does NOT: revert anything already merged, stop a running agent session mid-task, or
# affect human commits. It is a gate on landing, not a stop button on execution. If an
# agent session is actively doing something you want stopped, close the session.
#
# WHEN TO USE IT
#
# An agent did something you did not expect and you want to think. That is sufficient
# reason. Re-enabling costs one commit; leaving it on while you investigate costs
# nothing. Do not treat flipping it as an escalation — treat it as a pause.
#
# See kb/operations/runbooks/disable-autonomy.md
